 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Eviction: two queues (2Q)
+
+Replacement tracks two queues over the admitted sheets, both accounted in bytes of cached JPEG data; in-memory queue bookkeeping is not counted against the budget:
+
+- **Probation queue** (FIFO): a completed render is admitted to probation.
+- **Main queue** (LRU): a hit on a sheet while it is in probation promotes it to the main queue. A promoted sheet stays in the main queue until evicted.
+
+The 12 GiB budget is split between the two queues at a tunable byte boundary. An admission or promotion that would push its target queue past its share evicts entries from that queue — the probation FIFO tail, or the main queue's least-recently-used entry — until the sheet fits. Queue bookkeeping covers admitted sheets only; a pending job holds no queue state until its sheet completes and is admitted.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q: probation FIFO plus main LRU, promotion on re-access | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | A long sequential screening evicts the comparison working set under plain LRU, even though LRU is the cheapest to run. Aged frequency counters protect repeatedly popular clips but add per-hit counter maintenance and keep a production's favorites resident after editors switch projects. 2Q protects a sheet only after a second access, so one-off screening loads age out in probation while comparison sheets survive big screenings; the accepted costs are two queues and a split to tune, all private to the preview worker. |
+| Queue accounting | Bytes of cached JPEG data | Entry counts | The 12 GiB bound applies to cached JPEG data, and sheets are ~2 MiB but not uniform; count-based shares would not hold the byte bound. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
